KZ3 patch 1122 (SPU pass 104, upstream candidate): lv2 sync semantics for sys_mutex/sys_cond and the HLE
sys_lwmutex/sys_lwcond. Idea credit: the Ratchet & Clank port (their patch 7007 and notes:
lv2 hands the bound mutex to the woken waiter); MUTEX_WATCH idea from upstream PR #474 (erikomis, MIT; idea only, no
code taken). Semantics per lv2 as RPCS3 models it (read as a reference, no code copied).
New runtime/syscalls/lv2_sync_core.h: an lv2-style mutex (owner thread id, recursion count, recursive attribute, FIFO
queue of sleepers under one host lock) with bound condition queues. unlock hands the mutex to the oldest sleeper; a
condition signal moves the chosen waiters to the mutex (free -> owned as the signal returns, lwcond mode 3; held ->
handed over at the holder's unlock, sys_cond_signal and lwcond mode 1), so the woken waiter never re-locks it and no
third thread can take it in a gap (the Ratchet & Clank port's level-load stall). A timed-out condition wait takes the mutex back at its
old depth and returns ETIMEDOUT. MUTEX_WATCH=1 makes infinite waits print their owner every 5 s.
sys_mutex.c/sys_cond.c: built on the core (CRITICAL_SECTION / pthread mutex and the re-lock in sys_cond_wait gone);
destroy is ESRCH, EBUSY while owned or waited on, EPERM while a cond is bound. owner_tid/lock_count stay as mirrors.
ppu_sysprx.cpp: sys_lwmutex on the core keyed by the guest EA (the binary semaphore gone): busy trylock is EBUSY
0x8001000A (was 0x8001000B), the recursive attribute is stored (+0x08 = protocol | recursive) and a non-recursive
re-lock is EDEADLK, destroy is EBUSY while owned or waited on; sys_lwcond keeps patch 1059's FIFO order (signal =
oldest, signal_all = all queued, signal_to = named else oldest) with the hand-off, destroy ESRCH/EBUSY, wait without
the lwmutex EPERM; real on POSIX too (the POSIX wait was a no-op). Kept from the old code: a non-owner lwmutex unlock
still releases (logged [LWM-XUNLOCK]), over-release is CELL_OK, the [LWM-BLOCK]/[LWM-GOT]/PS3_LWMUTEX_TRACE logs.
New logs (first 16 each): [LWM-XUNLOCK], [LWC-EPERM], [LWM-DESTROY-BUSY]. runtime/ppu/kz3_lwcond_fifo.h (1059) is no
longer included (its host test still builds it).
KZ3 RE (EBOOT 1.00): the 3 sys_lwmutex_trylock callers (0x00CBFB68, 0x00CBFD4C, 0x00D32664) test zero/non-zero only;
sys_lwmutex_destroy callers assert on a non-zero result ("Mutex operation failed!"); no sys_cond syscalls (105-110),
sys_mutex 100/101/102/104 only. Host tests: tools/spu/test_r104_lv2_sync.sh (all FAIL without this patch, PASS
with; also runs the toolkit's tests/sync_stress suite). Notes the RE notes Pass 104.

Rebased on ps3recomp v0.18.0 (SPU pass 108): ours (lv2 core) over #577 in ppu_sysprx.cpp and over upstream's sys_mutex/sys_cond (#474 MUTEX_WATCH=1 idea already in 1122; upstream MUTEX_WATCH=2 and its destroy EBUSY are covered/lost: ours refuses destroy too). Upstream's HLE fast path (9a3fbffd, `ps3_hle_register_fast`) kept, `sys_lwmutex_lock_fast` rewritten on the lv2 core (trylock under the slot lock; held or queued = full handler). `ppu_thread_note_block` (PS3_PRIO_START, opt-in) not called from the lv2 core waits.

